+> STOP. Captain's notes: non-binding. Captain writes, Captain trims. Anyone else: close this file now.
+
+# Captain Notes
+
+Binding behaviour lives in `.feature` specs and referenced `assets/**`. History lives in git. These notes carry only what the next cycle needs.
+
+## Defect diagnosis (from the fixed, correct Cypress acceptance suite)
+
+The real-browser acceptance suite reports five failures that the in-harness Cucumber/happy-dom
+suite does not reproduce:
+
+1-2. "Mark all as completed" — toggle-all complete and toggle-all clear.
+3-4. "Item" — mark and un-mark a single item complete.
+5. "Editing" — the toggle must be hidden while a todo is being edited.
+
+Root cause for 1-4 is a DOM-identity defect: `render()` in `js/app.js` rebuilds the list with
+`todoList.innerHTML = ...` on every state change, so every interaction detaches and recreates the
+`<li>` nodes. A browser (Cypress) that holds a reference before the click sees the old node detach,
+so `cy.eq()` / `cy.find()` fail. happy-dom preserves node identity, so a step that captures a
+reference and compares it after the interaction DOES reproduce this in-harness; the new scenarios
+in `features/todo-dom-stability.feature` pin that.
+
+Failure 5 is visibility-only: there is no rule hiding the toggle (or the rest of the controls) while
+`.editing` is set, so the toggle stays visible. The new scenario pins "toggle not visible during
+editing" via `checkVisibility()`, which happy-dom honours for `display:none`.
+
+## Decision
+
+Pin all five as product-behaviour scenarios in `features/todo-dom-stability.feature` and list them in
+`watchbill.json` (watch1: identity for 1-4; watch2: editing visibility for 5). The Quartermaster
+authors the three new step definitions; the Crew fixes `js/app.js` to mutate list elements in place
+and to hide the toggle during edit. No production code written here.
+
+## Open question
+
+Failure 5's scenario fails on current code in-harness (toggle is visible). Whether it also turns
+green after a CSS-class fix depends on whether the harness applies the stylesheet for `checkVisibility`;
+the real-browser acceptance suite is the authority for that behaviour. If the in-harness cannot see a
+CSS-based fix, that is the expected browser-only limitation; do not force it.
